    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d31">

                <pb facs="0074" n="20" sameAs="frus:frus1944Quebec/pg20"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Hopkins Papers: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The President’s Special Assistant</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Hopkins</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to Prime Minister
                        Churchill</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1944-08-28">August 28, 1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>51. Personal and secret to the Prime Minister from Harry L. Hopkins.</p>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Winston</hi>: I am feeling ever so much better
                    but am still taking things pretty easy and I am inclined to think that I will
                    not risk the dangers of a setback by fighting the Battle of Quebec. Better men
                    than I have been killed there.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d31-f1"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Harry</hi></signed><anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d31-f2"/></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d31-f1" n="1" xml:id="d31-f1-content">
                <p>For Churchill’s interpretation of Hopkins’ absence from the Second Quebec
                    Conference, see Churchill, <hi rendition="#italic">Triumph and Tragedy</hi>, pp.
                    149, 161. Cf. Sherwood, p. 814.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d31-f2" n="2" xml:id="d31-f2-content">
                <p>The source text also has the holograph signature “Harry L Hopkins”, but only the
                    given name “Harry” was to be transmitted at the end of the message.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
